f 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471529} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04114d1-42d1-5aca-93b9-41b8ea74c83a" :answer-field/key "$h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03ec5353-d045-5242-94cf-0882c6a84756" :question/math-academy-id "q-77455" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- “$f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- “$g:{-4,-2,0,2,4,8,10}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* an injection. For example, $g(-4)=16=g(4),$ but $-4\\ne 4.$\n\n- “$h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$” is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(1),$ but $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051035 :topic/id #uuid "7c0a3263-cc36-57b6-839b-6ff5594cae61" :topic/math-academy-id 2678 :topic/title "Injections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choi