wer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1453da03-093a-550c-8d40-7a35a58b953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471739} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fedc310-6dba-54ed-b29f-2b1a2e3131b5" :answer-field/key "The function is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94a22ac-b31f-54dc-b35c-ba2dd08bc10e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82327" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef54b0fed6fa137d1a69b35c9b0231bf21192ca062fef6246df86a32591d0c0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={e}^{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function is an injection” is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/bd/bda8097fd869dfc60ccdd2dd42d38072248ad91a94853474461d0454c30e8027.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement “The function is a surjection” is false. Indeed, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “The function is a bijection” is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is “The function is an injection” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f049fe-3dd5-51ea-b497-14560ab72a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471293} {:answer/id #uuid "449a440c-b8f6-5ba8-916d-314262385167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f049fe-3dd5-51ea-b497-14560ab72a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5a451be-3277-52b0-b3b9-5ed4fa9ec66c" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∃$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236782ec-1004-5c4e-b908-84cbaed85b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471295} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb6b8c1-c39b-5a07-b965-549f553c2fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "236782ec-1004-5c4e-b908-84cbaed85b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471295} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4246827-7275-54df-9a91-9ed9cd495056" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $x$ in $2-x=w$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095be448-667d-5c3e-9af3-a8238f83781b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471297} {:answer/id #uuid "7c48180b-ef91-55a6-8173-f4bb51d2485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c48180b-ef91-55a6-8173-f4bb51d2485b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471298} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7526d36-196d-56b8-94dc-a37daca6a4b7" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cba8b56-135d-52f2-8f27-ede5f8ef30f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183619" :question/problem "$$\n∃{x}\\in R,\\,2-{x}={w}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ immediately after $∃$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $x$ in $2-x=w$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overbrace{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}^{\\text{scope of }∃x}\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $w$ on the right side of $2-x=w$” only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050691 :topic/id #uuid "5f607ed3-b581-5e92-9101-c32dd179ab13" :topic/math-academy-id 2792 :topic/title "Nested Quantifiers"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f71a22-fc9f-552f-a9d9-a46f8dcfc90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471299} {:answer/id #uuid "117e231e-8623-5e21-a59b-11496fbded59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f71a22-fc9f-552f-a9d9-a46f8dcfc90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471299} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c83e2c-3e5c-5076-8593-19b655c11223" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $m$ immediately after $∃$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27230c26-6810-56a0-8bd7-ff8d43922f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471301} {:answer/id #uuid "114c9caf-15ed-5900-b23a-3372178c16fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114c9caf-15ed-5900-b23a-3372178c16fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471302} :answer-field/id #uuid "6665bd07-59a9-5629-865d-a495f3ad2954" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $n$ on the left side of $n\\mid m$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7d0231-205c-56fd-9ea2-2ddb3e99793c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471303} {:answer/id #uuid "44987af5-9d26-5f40-b084-bcb86291212a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b7d0231-205c-56fd-9ea2-2ddb3e99793c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471303} :answer-field/id #uuid "4054af6c-64c2-5da3-8ab1-f1c077741993" :answer-field/key "The occurrence of $m$ on the right side of $n\\mid m$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55254c80-6a13-5f2c-a4bb-f9bcddde8c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183649" :question/problem "$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}\\in Z,\\,{n}\\,|\\,{m}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- The occurrence of $m$ immediately after $∃$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,n\\,|\\,m\n$$\n\n- The occurrence of $n$ on the left side of $n\\mid m$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- The occurrence of $m$ on the right side of $n\\mid m$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃m$ that uses $m:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,m\\in Z,\\,\\,\\overbrace{n\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}^{\\text{scope of }∃m}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is “The occurrence of $n$ on the left side of $n\\mid m$” only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051050 :topic/id #uuid "7d38602f-8b8c-581c-9835-2c6140959f96" :topic/math-academy-id 261 :topic/title "Coterminal Angles"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feade18a-2e4c-5097-902c-711c4760cc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471377} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b889a6-9642-51fa-95e6-36a5fa1c5dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b889a6-9642-51fa-95e6-36a5fa1c5dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b81048-0943-5c77-a333-7ca441be4347" :answer-field/key "$4\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a90230-6f59-512f-830c-e97128e86cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471379} {:answer/id #uuid "65dcd0b9-9263-58cd-90eb-bca20a23cfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a90230-6f59-512f-830c-e97128e86cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471379} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5e9d01-a48e-5e5e-8f17-d954e91b8970" :answer-field/key "$5\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90006613-87ac-5f3e-8870-d7a821f06782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471381} {:answer/id #uuid "3013e733-3914-5bb6-86a8-a7835d0676c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3013e733-3914-5bb6-86a8-a7835d0676c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471382} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2679b75-2746-5f95-aef4-73767c3bded7" :answer-field/key "$-2\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72a396d7-e8cf-5458-9d0a-c10ca03427ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-22984" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882 :topic/id #uuid "1ad374f3-e4f4-5dcc-ab6d-d6aed0790f3a" :topic/math-academy-id 1900 :topic/title "Level Curves"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871d9336-19b9-50e0-bd77-0ed2445f9632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471465} {:answer/id #uuid "75776144-4d99-5b5f-b7cf-e8dd702625ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "871d9336-19b9-50e0-bd77-0ed2445f9632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471465} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d51da3-8a0a-52eb-af91-f33f62f3fbf7" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives an ellipse" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd5f287-ae86-588f-93c5-3e92c682cfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471467} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b88c60-dc4a-5616-950a-c3e38c00242e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b88c60-dc4a-5616-950a-c3e38c00242e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471468} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e33b7b-f740-5166-aa3a-43e201713b8e" :answer-field/key "$c=2$ gives two lines" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6e7e-5ad4-5acf-80a9-86cf9a9ef12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471469} {:answer/id #uuid "14887bfd-0dfd-5db8-a62b-d3da3cadabbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6e7e-5ad4-5acf-80a9-86cf9a9ef12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471469} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a0e4ee-1ed3-5a2e-b4ec-e0d5e4dec86d" :answer-field/key "$c=3$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89b4a109-08c5-568a-830b-41c72ba008a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71401" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement “$c=2$ gives two lines” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723 :topic/id #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" :topic/math-academy-id 1904 :topic/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "696a6ad3-0dd7-5244-9263-a107168d0fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574503} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0badc2-e495-5fb4-b79b-5ec71ede016d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "696a6ad3-0dd7-5244-9263-a107168d0fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574503} :answer-field/id #uuid "364b7a02-86ec-5b9f-9fe9-851b3ef8bdf4" :answer-field/key "Column 1: $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2971a61e-b264-5233-9243-32d908007c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574505} {:answer/id #uuid "c288b555-1bb1-5084-8f5d-6631940b3d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2971a61e-b264-5233-9243-32d908007c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574505} :answer-field/id #uuid "d872e85c-47b0-5295-bead-af3322a16780" :answer-field/key "Column 2: $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be7e5596-eaa9-5760-92fe-6969a2c30957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574507} {:answer/id #uuid "6432bdff-20f3-5ea5-8a84-1f85f1a5b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6432bdff-20f3-5ea5-8a84-1f85f1a5b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574508} :answer-field/id #uuid "e156cf8b-65ed-5270-8734-8988651cee6e" :answer-field/key "Column 3: $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "882ebb4d-35c9-5b5b-8139-3566e490f853" :question/math-academy-id "q-73616" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $2$nd columns:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162 :topic/id #uuid "3308e65f-61fe-550b-8dc0-4e4a4925cb49" :topic/math-academy-id 799 :topic/title "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb0847e-f077-52b5-a2dc-3b5be04d8d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439947} {:answer/id #uuid "4427a1db-576b-5e6d-b443-4988d488b644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4427a1db-576b-5e6d-b443-4988d488b644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439948} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fddcc0-226d-57ff-8494-03c04378375d" :answer-field/key "$t=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c94317bf-98d7-5e75-83f2-d1c446fe74a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439949} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf1385-eb6b-5d99-a20e-9cfe7c73c596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94317bf-98d7-5e75-83f2-d1c446fe74a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439949} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0eb9e5-5bc8-5bf9-aee1-2aba7e4767ad" :answer-field/key "$t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b3b357c-4378-5fab-a768-e51431495981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439951} {:answer/id #uuid "5c56291f-1f8e-5f6b-a1a0-70bdda68135d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c56291f-1f8e-5f6b-a1a0-70bdda68135d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439952} :answer-field/id #uuid "04cd7793-0c8b-5ef8-b2ec-4c304a317b4b" :answer-field/key "$t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97350f2f-8344-5d51-ae1f-afa4912362a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439953} {:answer/id #uuid "9a443b41-52f7-5746-b3be-0b312d3c476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97350f2f-8344-5d51-ae1f-afa4912362a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439953} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8d9da9-bfdb-5788-8f6b-5523806704d2" :answer-field/key "$t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5eafad9-4c2d-5469-bbd9-3cd0fcca968a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75278" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18f0539-74a8-596c-852b-0d4f3da74f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471633} {:answer/id #uuid "da87614c-db0e-5e0d-85a3-4f809e951445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18f0539-74a8-596c-852b-0d4f3da74f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471633} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5174ed-11cb-5dac-8790-f507ecda6be1" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc7392ed-77ee-5baa-b9bc-16067d951374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471635} {:answer/id #uuid "fc39236e-b074-5bb4-8887-3fc17013ce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc39236e-b074-5bb4-8887-3fc17013ce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a26127c3-e674-5d2b-a403-9fdb7056fadf" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614463b9-6217-51ba-a62e-5077088acdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471637} {:answer/id #uuid "beab60f2-37b0-54a2-8506-e97f08da4b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614463b9-6217-51ba-a62e-5077088acdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471637} :answer-field/id #uuid "f171c56b-5039-5e7e-825f-8cf0186be360" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24df999b-6ea6-5095-ada6-d510b0d36076" :question/math-academy-id "q-80652" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$” only'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409637} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409656}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de855fe1-6aff-57db-86ff-8ce6879436d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471639} {:answer/id #uuid "90632757-8919-58b6-bfed-61661c939a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90632757-8919-58b6-bfed-61661c939a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471640} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9da46b-c58e-5af7-8e32-99a8a9fce27a" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{y}+3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1dca6b-130f-5024-a3e9-4606f7a93f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471641} {:answer/id #uuid "489d2221-0d68-5c98-9dcc-5350002296c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b1dca6b-130f-5024-a3e9-4606f7a93f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471641} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4cdfb6d-5811-5893-91cb-282a189e3dd2" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f426f513-c9ed-5df4-a21b-26cc46b017a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471643} {:answer/id #uuid "ef52d1b0-46f6-5a4f-9dde-6750a336e7d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f426f513-c9ed-5df4-a21b-26cc46b017a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471643} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f083bf-96db-5c32-aea8-1d7b3455b6ab" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)$ $=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ $+xy$ $+5$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ea0748a-6bf5-5641-b2b0-ad6357ffcd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80757" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y)=3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{y}+3x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+xy+5)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{y}+3x$” only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af458924-ca01-5898-a217-ad5d553da3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471645} {:answer/id #uuid "14fc048d-3f9d-5fc2-a10d-6ad474656201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af458924-ca01-5898-a217-ad5d553da3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471645} :answer-field/id #uuid "32b29457-96e6-5716-9c8f-72d05a3781ae" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x-2{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y-2xy$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422a66d0-0514-59b6-bae8-0a82156e01de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471647} {:answer/id #uuid "afe0f26c-1cc5-5fd5-9dcb-54c08a45e634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe0f26c-1cc5-5fd5-9dcb-54c08a45e634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471648} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ce32ea-0386-50f2-ae3a-34ea801fc895" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f08cd5-2d98-52c5-ac7d-ff635246ed1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471649} {:answer/id #uuid "c48f4e61-0f8e-5507-8ade-96cded5bbd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48f4e61-0f8e-5507-8ade-96cded5bbd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471650} :answer-field/id #uuid "be671fcc-b41f-5041-b580-f3ac5796e945" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "813fc330-f1d0-5e6c-9e4f-df4f6cf7d2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80762" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-2{y}^{2})=-4y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-2xy)=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y})=6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$” and “${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y}$” only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459 :topic/id #uuid "a163637a-ed19-547a-85e8-0acfdb20fa0f" :topic/math-academy-id 865 :topic/title "Introduction to Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8c65cd4-8483-5860-b7e4-2e5f6999dce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470901} {:answer/id #uuid "d058bcfd-95da-5b43-97d7-abe7d98ec345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058bcfd-95da-5b43-97d7-abe7d98ec345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470902} :answer-field/id #uuid "161e0852-2b45-54f8-8b42-267cc6b1c63c" :answer-field/key "$T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d386c399-9111-5bbd-9887-033eb7004c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470903} {:answer/id #uuid "69c4a485-5228-57d4-979a-ab95a231931b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69c4a485-5228-57d4-979a-ab95a231931b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470904} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48a1d31-3b71-53aa-a009-2fca300bedeb" :answer-field/key "$T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf59e0b8-6c9c-5268-9247-b6aa262b7519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470905} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f1a31-c756-5d23-affa-6305848f8743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f1a31-c756-5d23-affa-6305848f8743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470906} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7620bf-1e26-5a27-9705-fa63bd75423e" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a linear transformation" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3861b-a381-5b7c-bc56-9232209a631c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12008" :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049363 :topic/id #uuid "eefd5285-6b99-569d-a5bb-365f176fa757" :topic/math-academy-id 807 :topic/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c2e0a46-a4ee-5819-bfd8-b6605a0d8094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439879} {:answer/id #uuid "e9369708-bcb2-5c13-a3f7-152ce020d3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2e0a46-a4ee-5819-bfd8-b6605a0d8094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439879} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca3d5ff2-3ae3-5582-b549-bdcb6611dd13" :answer-field/key "$x=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffb34ed7-9fe8-5948-babf-545c5e4e0736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439881} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d04f5d-ce83-59f2-88c7-83f4627e68e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb34ed7-9fe8-5948-babf-545c5e4e0736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439881} :answer-field/id #uuid "012b94fc-d8bf-510d-a520-9dfc6c1811ab" :answer-field/key "$x=- \\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440298} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f170be1-ffb8-592e-bb80-9f0195579084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439883} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba98035-0918-59db-93dd-e795d4f5ebd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f170be1-ffb8-592e-bb80-9f0195579084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439883} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c94165b-9d14-56fa-8e1c-3541dde89a56" :answer-field/key "$x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "547ea0a2-9056-5429-bd0f-633e68fa5943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439885} {:answer/id #uuid "1638a4a5-ff97-5f43-b834-d3d67a690eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547ea0a2-9056-5429-bd0f-633e68fa5943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439885} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89178d8-8578-52ac-9dd9-ce525bf7df93" :answer-field/key "$x=\\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440300} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "965e8857-7f8e-5f00-aa12-bd8820ca46e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439887} {:answer/id #uuid "f7855e38-b957-5e31-90eb-6ce83b7a223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "965e8857-7f8e-5f00-aa12-bd8820ca46e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439887} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1222c91-f556-5bcf-9084-5d1456dda102" :answer-field/key "$x=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049761 :topic/id #uuid "119f6367-1904-5d42-b538-ea1a0ba89992" :topic/math-academy-id 852 :topic/title "Finding the Center and Axes of Ellipses by Completing the Square"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf85bac6-b89d-5c73-adb5-4938e4b8f922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471519} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5a20bb-c60f-5338-87c9-e2490e64f006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e5a20bb-c60f-5338-87c9-e2490e64f006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471520} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c23e7a2-a53b-5060-bbaf-9b3bfe5e7a0f" :answer-field/key "Its center is $(1,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b974d4-3fd9-51e3-bc6c-5f28cf8e13fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471521} {:answer/id #uuid "ece7e85c-4f4f-5b66-afe1-c4c1f94870d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b974d4-3fd9-51e3-bc6c-5f28cf8e13fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471521} :answer-field/id #uuid "844f18a7-507e-59d5-b3d2-aaaf4e9e2971" :answer-field/key "The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ef67bd-4095-53fe-94d3-afa4b2bac162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471523} {:answer/id #uuid "98e15137-ba00-5798-bf72-ba42aba63f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98e15137-ba00-5798-bf72-ba42aba63f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471524} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc3abea-1ce4-58a0-a0ef-8401134def94" :answer-field/key "The length of the minor axis is $4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2cdae2-5e26-5778-ab7e-65d79d794e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-7639" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  “Its center is $(1,-3)$” and “The length of the minor axis is $4$” are true, and “The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$” is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/id #uuid "8c876864-a853-56d6-a887-91d7f8f1cf14" :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fed243-4084-56d8-84b0-478adfaa2bb0" :answer/type {:db/id 